%% ================================================================ PREFACE
\section*{Introduction}

\initial{V}{ox in Rama} is the communion of the Mass of the Holy Innocents,
28 December. Mikołaj Zieleński set it for four voices and organ, and it was
printed in Venice in 1611 as no.\,36 of his \la{Communiones totius anni}
(‘Communions for the whole year’). The \la{Communiones} were issued together
with his \la{Offertoria totius anni} (‘Offertories for the whole year’) as
a single publication with continuous signatures. Between them the two books
hold all 113 of Zieleński’s known works.

Little is known about the composer. The title pages call him ‘Polonus’ (‘a Pole’) and
‘organarius et capellae magister’ (‘organist and chapel master’) to Wojciech Baranowski, archbishop of
Gniezno and primate of Poland from 1608 until his death in 1615. Documents
gathered by Polish scholars add that he came from Warka and married Anna
Feterówna. In 1604 Baranowski, then bishop of Płock, gave him land at Gromin
near Pułtusk. He appeared in court at Łowicz, the primate’s seat, at the turn
of February and March and again in April 1611. His dates of birth and death
are unknown, and the ‘1550–1615’ printed in library catalogues and on modern
scores is a convention, not a documented span. Szymon Starowolski wrote in
1625 that Zieleński had studied in Rome. No document confirms it. The
dedication of the 1611 books is dated ‘Venetijs Kalend. Martij’ (‘at Venice, on the Kalends of March’), on a day
when the Łowicz records place him in Poland, so it says nothing about where he
was.

The dedication does explain why the books were made. Baranowski, it says, had
restored order in his dioceses and ordered that ‘this proper singing of the
offertories and communions, each in its place and season’ be exactly observed
(\la{hanc ipsam certam ac propriam Offertoriorum, ac Communionum suis locis et
temporibus decantationem exactè obseruari imperasti}). He commissioned the
work and paid for the printing. Bound with the parts is a calendar, the
\la{Registrum}, that assigns an offertory and a communion to each feast.
Agnieszka Leszczyńska calls it unprecedented in prints of polyphony. It lists
\la{Vox in Rama} for the Innocents and again for their octave on 4 January.
Zieleński also claims a first. These, he writes, are offertories and
communions ‘composed for the first time by a Pole in the new manner’
(\la{primùm à Polono nouo modo concinnata}).

The \la{Communiones} are ordered by scoring, not by the calendar. Pieces for
one voice and organ come first, then pieces for two and three voices with
instruments, then the pieces for four, five and six voices. \la{Vox in Rama}
is the fourth of eleven four-voice pieces. A rubric in the index governs all
the pieces from four voices up: those that go plainly (\la{simpliciter}) are
to be sung with the organ and played on instruments; those written with
ornaments (\la{cum resolutione}) are sung by the singers with the organ played
softly. \la{Vox in Rama} is one of the plain pieces. The catalogue of Kurtzman
and Schnoebelen, made from the Wrocław and Kraków books, records a further
rubric for it: ‘Soli debent cantare cum Organo’ (‘they are to sing alone
with the organ’).

\subsection{The text} The communion takes Matthew 2:18, where the evangelist
quotes Jeremiah 31:15 after the slaughter of the children of Bethlehem. The
Missal of 1570 gives it as ‘Vox in Rama audita est, ploratus \& ululatus:
Rachel plorans filios suos: \& noluit consolari, quia non sunt’. This is the
Vulgate text of Matthew without \la{multus}, which there reads ‘ploratus, et
ululatus multus’ (‘weeping and great wailing’). Jeremiah’s own Vulgate wording
is different: ‘Vox in excelso
audita est lamentationis, luctus, et fletus Rachel plorantis filios suos’
(‘a voice was heard on high, of lamentation, of mourning and of weeping, of
Rachel weeping for her children’).
The feast was kept as a day of mourning inside Christmastide. The general
rubrics of the same Missal prescribe violet vestments for the Innocents
unless the day falls on a Sunday.

\subsection{The music} The setting is 45 breves long and follows the text
phrase by phrase. Each voice states ‘Vox in Rama’ two or three times in
staggered entries, often adding a further ‘in Rama’, and the long melismas
fall on the stressed ‘Ra-’. ‘Audita est’ is set almost syllabically. The
chromatic writing is kept for ‘ploratus et ululatus’. In bar 15 the Cantus
falls c″–b′–b\fl′, and the b\fl′ sounds over the Altus f\sh′ and the Tenor
d′, an augmented triad on ‘ululatus’. In bar 18 the Cantus has
g″–f\sh″–f″. ‘Rachel plorans’ and ‘filios suos’ are short imitative points.
‘Et noluit consolari’ comes four times in the Altus and Tenor and three times
in the Cantus and Bassus. ‘Quia non sunt’ is set twice, the second time over
a Bassus d held for two breves, and the piece closes on a chord of A with a
major third.

The text had been set often as a motet, by Clemens non Papa for four voices,
for example, and by Giaches de Wert for five (printed 1581). Zieleński wrote
it as the proper of the day: short, close to the words, and with the organ
beneath the voices.

\section*{Sources}

% TITLE PAGE (principles 3.9): line ends ' | ' not yet marked; they need the
% title page itself (Printed Sacred Music records 3388-3389, or the MMP D
% facsimile). For Miki.
\subsection{The print} \la{Commvniones totivs anni quibus in solennioribus
festis Sancta Romana Ecclesia uti consueuit ad cantum Organi, per Vnam, Duas,
Tres, Quatuor, Quinque, \& Sex voces} … Venetiis, Apud Iacobum Vincentium.
MDCXI (RISM A/I Z\,200, issued with Z\,199). Quarto partbooks and a folio
\la{Partitura pro organo}. The only complete set came from the church of
St Bernardine in Wrocław and was in the city library until 1942. Half of it
was lost in the war. What survives:

\begin{sigla}
\item[PL-WRu] Wrocław, Biblioteka Uniwersytecka, 50914 Muz. (formerly
  Stadtbibliothek Breslau, Mus. 777): Cantus primi chori, Tenor primi
  chori, Altus secundi chori, Tenor secundi chori.
\item[PL-Kc] Kraków, Biblioteka Książąt Czartoryskich: \la{Partitura pro
  organo}; no shelfmark known. According to the Musicare database it gives
  some pieces in full score.
\item[PL-Kj] Kraków, Biblioteka Jagiellońska, Cim. Qu. 5822: a fragment of
  the Cantus.
\end{sigla}

\noindent A fragment of an Altus book was in the Baworowski Library in Lwów
until the Second World War; where it is now is not known.

The four voices of no.\,36 were printed in the books of the first choir. The
Tenor primi chori survives and is the source of this edition’s Tenor. The
Cantus primi chori also survives and should hold the Cantus, but its
facsimile was not available. The Altus and Bassus were printed in the
Altus and Bassus primi chori, which are lost.
Before the war Zdzisław Jachimecki and Maria Szczepańska transcribed the
complete set, and every later edition rests on their copies.

\begin{sigla}
\siglum{T} Tenor primi chori, no.\,36: ‘A 4. In festo
  Sanctorum Innocentium. 36 TENOR’ (‘For 4. On the feast of the Holy
  Innocents. 36 Tenor’). Six staves; C2 clef, sign cut-C; text
  placed under the notes, with \textit{ij} for repeated words. Wrocław,
  Biblioteka Uniwersytecka, 50914 Muz. Read in the facsimile,
  \textit{Monumenta Musicae in Polonia}, ser.\,D, vol.\,12, ed. Jerzy Morawski
  (Kraków: PWM, 1983): Chicago Public Library, M2014.6 .Z5 O4 1983,
  digitised by the Internet Archive. The facsimile was retouched by its
  editors, so a reading that rests on a fine mark (the e\na′ of bar 15)
  needs checking against the original. \textit{Base text for the Tenor.}
\siglum{N} \la{Vox in Rama}, notAmos Performing Editions, full score 100881
  (PDF, 2020; CPDL). Four voices and a keyboard reduction. Final E, one-sharp
  signature, note values halved, barred in a mixture of 3/2 and 2/2.
\siglum{M} \la{Vox in Rama}, ed. Paul R. Marchesano (Finale PDF, 2009, and
  MusicXML, 2017; CPDL). Final F, four-flat signature, note values halved,
  barred in 4/4. \textit{Base text for Cantus, Altus and Bassus.}
\end{sigla}

\noindent \sig{N} and \sig{M} agree in every note, rest and accidental. \sig{M} lies a
semitone above \sig{N}, and the two are barred differently. Their underlay
differs in two places (bars 5–6 and 38–39). Both name the 1611 print as their
source, but neither can have been made from the partbooks alone, since the
Altus and Bassus are lost. They share readings that \sig{T} shows to be
editorial: the e\fl′ of bar 15, which \sig{T} prints as e\na′, and the four
sharps that \sig{T} lacks (bars 8, 25, 31). They also share the Altus an
octave low in bars 17–18. So one was copied from the other, or both descend
from a common parent: WDMP\,12, Malinowski, or the \la{Partitura} in
facsimile (principles 3.5). Their agreement counts as one witness, not two. \sig{M} was chosen as base text
because it exists as encoded data.

\subsection{Editions not seen} Adolf Chybiński and Bronisław Rutkowski edited
\la{Vox in Rama} as Wydawnictwo Dawnej Muzyki Polskiej 12 (Warsaw: TWMP, 1933;
reissued by PWM in 1964, 1971 and 1980) ‘wg druku z r.\,1611’ (‘from the print of 1611’), while the print
was still complete. The library catalogue gives its scoring as ‘2 soprani,
mezzosoprano e tenore [con organo]’ (‘2 sopranos, mezzo-soprano and tenor
[with organ]’), which matches the ranges at the printed
pitch. Władysław Malinowski’s \textit{Opera omnia} in \textit{Monumenta
Musicae in Polonia}, ser.\,A (1966–91), has the communions in vols.\,4–5. The
facsimiles of the Cantus primi chori (MMP D/11) and the \la{Partitura}
(MMP D/15) have not been seen.

\section*{Editorial method}
\seriesrules

\subsection{Status of the text} The Tenor is edited from the 1611 print. The
other three voices come from \sig{M}, a modern edition at least two steps removed
from the print. \sig{T} shows how far such an edition can be trusted. Its notes
and rhythms agree with \sig{M} everywhere, once \sig{M}’s transposition and halved values
are undone. Its accidentals do not: of the thirteen chromatic notes in \sig{M}’s
Tenor, eight are printed in \sig{T}, four are sharps supplied by the parent
edition, and one, the e\fl′ in bar 15, is an e\na′ in the print. Readers should
assume that the accidentals of the Cantus, Altus and Bassus include editorial
ones in about the same proportion. One reading of \sig{M} is emended, in the
Altus at bars 17–18 (see the notes).

\subsection{Pitch, values and barring} As in \sig{T}. The Tenor’s C2 clef puts
the piece on A with no signature, a fourth above \sig{N} and a major third above
\sig{M}. Note values are those of \sig{T}, twice those of \sig{N} and \sig{M}. The sign cut-C is
\sig{T}’s; the edition assumes it stood in every voice. A bar is one breve, the
tactus. The incipit before the Tenor gives the clef, sign and first note of
\sig{T}; the clefs of the other voices are not known, and they have no incipit.
The range of each voice is shown at the head of its staff. The final notes are
longae, as in \sig{T}.

\subsection{Voices and clefs} At the printed pitch the ranges are Cantus
g′–a″, Altus d′–f″ (after the emendation in bars 17–18), Tenor g–a′ and
Bassus A–c′. With a Tenor in C2 they point to a high set of clefs, perhaps
G2, G2 or C1, C2, F3, which is what the WDMP scoring ‘2 soprani,
mezzosoprano e tenore’ (‘2 sopranos, mezzo-soprano and tenor’) describes. Cantus and Altus are a pair of equal
voices: they enter on the same e″, share a range and cross often. The Cantus
cannot belong an octave lower. There it would lie below the Altus at nine in
ten note onsets and below the Tenor at more than half of them, while at the
printed octave it never goes below the Tenor. The Cantus and Altus are
printed in the treble clef, the Tenor in the octave treble clef, the Bassus
in the bass clef.

\subsection{Sounding pitch} High clefs were a way of writing, not a pitch to
be sung. Adriano Banchieri’s \textit{Cartella} (Venice, 1601; 2nd edn 1610,
pp.\,13–15) states the rule that pieces in the G clef are sung a fourth lower
when they have a flat and a fifth lower when they have none: ‘quando sono per
b.\,molle si trasportano … una Quarta bassa, \& quando sono per b.\,quadro, si
trasportano similmente una Quinta bassa’ (‘when they are in \textit{b molle}
they are transposed … a fourth lower, and when they are in \textit{b quadro}
they are likewise transposed a fifth lower’). His written-out example of the
fifth-lower case has its Tenor in C2, the clef of \sig{T}. Silverio Picerli gives
the same rule in 1631. Both are quoted, with Banchieri’s example, by Andrew
Johnstone (2006). \la{Vox in Rama} has no signature, so by this rule it
sounded a fifth lower, ending on D: Cantus c′–d″, Altus g–b\fl′, Tenor c–d′,
Bassus D–f, an ordinary four-part choir. The organ basses that Palestrina’s
publishers printed for his high-clef works follow the same rule, a fifth
lower without a flat and a fourth lower with one. Praetorius (1619) allows a
fourth for some modes, and later Italian practice moved towards the fourth
(Doni, 1640; Pasquini, 1695). The CPDL editions take it down a fourth, to E.
A fourth down cannot be written in the notation of 1611 without a one-sharp
signature, while a fifth down gives D with one flat, an ordinary notation of
the time. The \la{Partitura} may settle the question, since organ parts
sometimes printed the transposition. This edition prints the written pitch;
the performance edition is a fifth lower.

\subsection{Accidentals} In the Tenor, accidentals before notes are those of
\sig{T}, and accidentals above the staff are editorial. In the other voices every
accidental is \sig{M}’s and stands before the note, whether the print had it or
not. Cautionaries in round brackets are editorial in every voice
(principles 7.3).

\subsection{Ligatures and coloration} The three ligatures of \sig{T}, all
\la{cum opposita proprietate}, are shown by brackets above the notes. In the
second (bar 17) the second note is black; corner brackets mark it.

\subsection{Underlay} \sig{T} places its text under the notes but loosely, and
writes \textit{ij} four times for a repeated phrase (bars 21–22, 33–35, 35–37,
43–45); there the Tenor's words are in angle brackets (principles 11.9). Its words fall where \sig{M}’s do. Where
\sig{M} puts a new syllable on a semiminim, it is kept if principles 10.1
allows it, and the critical note names the clause. In bar 12 of the Altus
the underlay departs from 10.1 for the singer’s sake, on the ground given in
the note. No word of the text is left out (principles 10.13), and every
statement of ‘et noluit’ is sung
syllabically, on the same rhythm in each voice (principles 10.6, 10.10).
One departure from the series rules is kept because the print shows it
(principles 10.12): most statements set the unstressed ‘con-’ of ‘consolari’
to a melisma of five to seven notes, and \sig{T}’s spacing, ‘con … sola-’,
shows the same. In bars 5–6 of the
Altus \sig{N}’s underlay is preferred, for the reason given in the notes.

\subsection{Text} \sig{T}’s ampersand is written \la{et}, and its v as u
(\la{ululatus} for \la{vlulatus}; principles 11.1). Punctuation follows the
Missal. The first word is capitalised; repeated ‘vox’ is not.

